      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean assumes every axiom of the first system is true in the standard natural numbers and asserts that every theorem of the enlarged system is true there. The passage speaks of producing a particular formula as true, without explicitly requiring or asserting global soundness. The target thus covers sound starting systems, not merely consistent ones."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lucas leaves the enlargement open. Lean chooses a particular one: retain all the old axioms and add just the first consistency sentence as an axiom. Recursive enumerability of this extension stands for the existence of the second machine."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The consistency-sentence proxy is used twice, not just for the first machine. The second machine’s unprovable sentence says that its own Craig presentation is consistent. This is specific to the enlarged system, as required for the claimed role, but it is not literally the self-referential sentence constructed by applying Gödel’s diagonal procedure to that system."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "On the stated role-based reading, the target captures the passage: the extension is again effectively axiomatized, proves the designated sentence that the first system could not, and has a designated unprovable sentence of its own. It does so for sound arithmetic systems using consistency sentences rather than literal Gödel sentences."
